namespace Dakota {

/** \page IteratorContextDesign IteratorContext Design Intent

\section IteratorContextPurpose Purpose

IteratorContext exists to package the runtime execution state for one
subordinate iterator relationship. It is the object stored by components
such as NestedModel and MetaIterator when they need to manage the execution
of a child iterator.

Its purpose is not to define what the child iterator means. Its purpose is
to remember how that child iterator should be configured and executed in the
current runtime environment.

\section IteratorContextResponsibilities Responsibilities

IteratorContext is responsible for carrying the execution state that must be
preserved across communicator lifecycle phases and execution phases.
Examples include partitioning-related state, scheduling mode, server
identity, active/idle rank role, and the executor configuration associated
with the subordinate iterator.

It provides the call surface used by orchestration components during the
lifecycle of subordinate execution. In current code this includes calls such
as update(), configure(), partition(), initialize_iterator(),
set_iterator(), free_iterator(), execute_iterator(), and
schedule_iterators().

This lets callers such as NestedModel keep ownership of semantic timing and
control flow while delegating the mechanics of subordinate execution to a
single helper object.

\section IteratorContextStable Stable Intent

The stable design intent is:

<ul>
<li>A component that owns a subordinate iterator should store a reusable
execution helper rather than re-deriving execution state ad hoc.</li>
<li>Execution state for subordinate iterator orchestration should survive
across init/set/free/evaluate-style phases.</li>
<li>Callers such as NestedModel should own semantic decisions and timing,
while IteratorContext owns subordinate execution state.</li>
</ul>

\section IteratorContextExpectedChange Expected Evolution

The exact distribution of behavior between IteratorContext, StudyRuntime,
and IteratorExecutor is expected to evolve.

Today IteratorContext is partly a state holder and partly a forwarding API.
Some of its methods directly call IteratorExecutor, while others route
through StudyRuntime. That layering is not yet perfectly regularized.
Future refactoring may simplify or rebalance those boundaries.

What is expected to remain stable is the role of IteratorContext as the
per-subordinate execution handle.

\section IteratorContextRelationship Relationship To NestedModel And MetaIterators

NestedModel and the meta-iterators still own the meaning of their
sub-studies. They decide when communicator initialization happens, when a
sub-iterator should execute synchronously, when jobs should be queued, and
how results are mapped back into their own semantics.

IteratorContext does not replace those responsibilities. Instead, it gives
those components one object that captures the execution setup for the child
iterator across the communicator lifecycle.

In this sense, IteratorContext is a runtime helper for orchestration code,
not a semantic study component in its own right.

\section IteratorContextSeeAlso See Also

See \ref RuntimeExecutionDesign for the subsystem overview.

*/

} // namespace Dakota
